+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

This port disables Lynx features that launch external programs,
including shell, editor, mail, and clipboard helpers. Local file access
is limited by an unveil(2) policy; arbitrary paths outside the current
directory and the listed state files may be unavailable. The current
directory is not unveiled when it is HOME or lies inside HOME, so run
Lynx from a directory you are willing to expose for that session.

Run Lynx as an unprivileged user with HOME set to an existing directory.
Startup refuses root and fails closed if HOME cannot be resolved. Lynx
creates a private temporary subdirectory and confines temporary files to
it. Set LYNX_SAVE_SPACE to an existing directory owned by you with mode
0700; other permissions and HOME itself are rejected. A real
HOME/Downloads directory is readable but not writable unless it is the
selected private save directory; a symlink there is left inaccessible.
The selected save directory remains writable. Do not use this sandboxed
package as root.

The sandbox is single-process and does not provide site isolation or a
separate renderer. The pledge/unveil rules constrain Lynx itself only.

OpenBSD TLS uses the base-system LibreSSL libraries and CA files. HTTPS
and secure NNTP connections verify the certificate chain and server
name. The force_ssl_prompt setting still controls explicit user
decisions about untrusted certificates; the default remains to reject
them.
